00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:09.110
LUCIEN AVRAMOV: In my
own words, modernization

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:14.630
is about basically
get on-premise, get

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:19.670
legacy virtualized
environments, and getting them

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:23.390
to you in a way where
you can actually run them

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:26.240
as containers, where
you can operate them

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:32.689
with less overhead for basically
programability upgrades,

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:36.690
patching, and how to run
in a cloud environment

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:40.970
in a more automatic way,
where you can start,

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:44.600
once you are there,
leveraging cloud products

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:48.270
and cloud tools that we offer.

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:50.550
So this is kind of our
view of modernization.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:54.620
So I would say if you take a
virtual machine as a starting

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.130
point and we get to an
endpoint which would be running

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:02.420
on a container and having
CI/CD pipeline instrumentation,

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:04.970
having monitoring,
troubleshooting tools,

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:09.170
real-time, that will show you
what your application is doing

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:10.910
with the rest of the world--

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:13.820
how many customers
are touching it,

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:18.350
how many connections per second,
who this application is talking

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:21.500
with, which other application
it interfaces with, what's

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.810
the latency, and so forth.

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:24.770
That's one part.

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:26.930
And then the other
part is, well, how

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:29.190
do I upgrade my application?

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:30.470
How do I patch it?

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:32.010
How do I move it?

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:34.470
How do I make sure it scales?

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.160
And so once you are
in this environment,

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:38.480
you are modernized.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:42.290
And so this is what
modernization means to me.

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:44.540
And now what I'd like
to cover is basically

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:49.832
to see why we're doing
it, why we're modernizing.

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:56.060
So enterprise IT is complex.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:56.937
Why is it complex?

00:01:56.937 --> 00:01:58.520
Because you're in
an environment where

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:03.170
you have multiple different
systems in your infrastructure.

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:04.910
You may have virtualized
environments.

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:06.170
You may have bare-metal.

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:10.419
You may have legacy middleware.

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:12.770
And basically you'll
end up in an environment

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:15.650
where you have a bunch
of things to make work.

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:19.100
And you need to make them evolve
because your task to evolve

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:19.830
this system.

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:23.608
So your initial starting
point is complicated.

00:02:23.608 --> 00:02:24.650
And that's what you have.

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:26.540
And that's your environment.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:30.410
And basically for your
company and your environment

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:32.690
to be responsive
and competitive,

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:34.700
it has to quickly evolve.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:36.620
If you have a new
application coming up,

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:39.030
a new service that you want
to offer to your customers,

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:41.830
you have to quickly do it.

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:43.820
And so on the one
hand, you're facing

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:46.340
the environment that exists.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:47.990
On the other hand,
there is a need

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:51.410
of more and more agility
into your environment,

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:56.150
faster and faster deployment
times for applications

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:57.000
and so forth.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.670
And so we started
being in a place where

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:00.500
we needed to do trade-offs.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:04.580
And the trade-offs are in three
different types, basically.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:07.740
One is about
security and agility.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:09.860
So you want to be agile,
but at the same time

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:12.650
you want your application
to be secure enough.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:16.520
And so you start to think
how to get more agile.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:18.740
But at the same time,
your security teams

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:22.748
would not want this to be
more impactful with the data.

00:03:22.748 --> 00:03:25.040
So you have to think about
these two, how they coexist.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:26.460
That's number one.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:30.080
Number two is
reliability and cost.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:34.970
So you want to basically have
an infrastructure where cost

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:37.020
is less and less and so forth.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.020
but at the same time,
you want it reliable.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:40.370
You don't want to have failures.

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:41.840
You don't want to have outages.

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:44.360
You want uptime in SLOs.

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:46.610
You want to make sure that
if you have customers using

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:49.940
your environment that are in
Asia, in Europe, in the US,

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:52.370
that based on who they
are and where they are,

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:55.220
they have a latency and
an application response

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:59.760
time that suits what you
want to provide to them.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:01.983
And same for internal
applications.

00:04:01.983 --> 00:04:04.400
So if you are hosting your
internal applications somewhere

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.550
on the cloud, you want to
make sure that A, it works,

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:09.710
and, two, that it
works well enough

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:13.350
with the proper performance.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:16.380
And then number three is
portability and consistency.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:21.209
So how can I move my application
to a different environment?

00:04:21.209 --> 00:04:25.620
How can I go from my
on-premise to the cloud?

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:27.990
How can I go back from the
cloud to an on-premise?

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:31.360
How can I have
multiple clouds coexist

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:33.620
and at the same time be agile?

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:35.585
If a new service
appears somewhere

00:04:35.585 --> 00:04:37.460
and I want my application
to benefit from it,

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:39.110
I'd like to move it there.

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:42.260
And so that's the
thinking of the trade-offs

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:45.920
that we need to do in order
to basically modernize

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:48.300
an environment.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:52.620
And basically what
we need to embrace

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:55.180
is how do we do new things?

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:59.730
How do we transform this
IT environment versus doing

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:01.170
the same thing somewhere else?

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:08.400
So what a CIO of a
media company found

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.980
is that 90% of
legacy apps would not

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:13.500
be optimized by
moving to the cloud.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:15.660
By just moving an
application, it

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.720
doesn't mean that you start
benefiting from all what

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:20.000
the cloud has to offer you.

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:26.750
And if you don't
modernize, what happens?

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:31.120
Well, you have
underutilized resources.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:33.160
You have turnover.

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:35.680
Some people may go somewhere
else, losing interest

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:37.180
and so forth.

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:39.220
Downtime-- you have
downtime as well

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:40.930
because you're not more modern.

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:43.348
You don't have a
24/7 reliability.

00:05:43.348 --> 00:05:44.890
You have to have
maintenance windows.

00:05:44.890 --> 00:05:47.230
You have to take
downtime to upgrade.

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:50.530
And so that's another
part of not modernizing.

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:52.240
And third, while
you may be locked

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:55.480
in with specific vendors
and not have the agility

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:57.920
to move when you want to move--

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:00.880
and there's more and more
this need to adapt yourself

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:03.190
to the environment
because there's so many

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:08.080
new ways to do business and
to do technical application

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:11.140
deployments and so many new
softwares and ways of doing

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:14.530
the same thing somewhere
else and in another ways

00:06:14.530 --> 00:06:15.730
that you want to be more--

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:18.520
you want to give yourself
the chances to succeed.

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:23.350
And this is why we're so much
thinking about modernization

00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:29.890
and why we're spending time on
this product for modernizing.

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:33.640
And so basically the fact
that you will modernize,

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:37.270
it's not that it's basically
an end of something

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:39.740
and you have to rebuild and
figure out how to do it.

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:43.420
It's basically a way
to find a different way

00:06:43.420 --> 00:06:49.780
to build your environment in
a modernized infrastructure.

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:53.230
And so the technologies
for modernization,

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:57.090
here I put the five
key ones that I see.

00:06:57.090 --> 00:07:00.580
Microservices-- what
does microservices mean?

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:02.920
Microservices means
the flexibility

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:09.160
to move at the speed that
your developers move.

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:14.920
Containers-- containers means
repetitive, repeatability and

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:15.750
scalability.

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:18.670
You can run something of--

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:20.890
you can have an
application that's portable

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:22.750
and that can be
repeated and moved

00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:27.250
to various places
in a faster way.

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:29.860
Orchestration-- what
does orchestration mean?

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:35.620
It means that we have systems
that will automate and move

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:37.330
these applications
where it makes

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:39.900
sense when it makes sense.

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:42.280
CI/CD, what is that?

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:45.880
That means that you will
be shipping code hourly,

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:47.710
and you will be agile.

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:49.520
You won't be on the
quarterly basis--

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:51.310
for example, the
development release.

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:55.230
You will just push the
code when it's available

00:07:55.230 --> 00:07:56.860
and when it's tested.

00:07:56.860 --> 00:08:01.750
And these are the services
and API management.

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:07.210
So automate your management
with basically having an API

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:10.570
framework where you will
touch other applications

00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:12.160
and you will have
a tight integration

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:15.850
between different parts
of your lifecycle,

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:19.270
different applications
in your lifecycle.

00:08:19.270 --> 00:08:20.860
So these are the
technologies that

00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:23.050
are part of this
modernization journey.

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:30.520
It is a big deal when we look at
customer surveys and basically

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:31.750
market surveys.

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:35.919
Migration and modernization
are a big deal

00:08:35.919 --> 00:08:39.280
what you see here is
migrating existing

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:44.650
applications to the cloud,
most people believe in that.

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:48.320
Evolving the application is
also important and top of mind.

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:51.430
So these are really
key aspects in terms

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:55.330
of modernizing
infrastructure, and that's

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:57.130
what we get from markets.

00:08:57.130 --> 00:09:03.430
Now, the problem with that is
that it's actually complicated.

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:04.140
It's hard.

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:05.140
It's a hard thing to do.

00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:09.200
Everyone wants to modernize,
but how do we do it?

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:15.640
And when we look at some more
and more data, more statistics,

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:19.780
over 90% of enterprises
migration projects

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:24.100
face delays over budget.

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:25.360
Why is it so hard?

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:27.340
Number one, the scale.

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:30.880
So you have thousands of
workloads, for example,

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:34.760
in a data center, and you need
to move that to the cloud.

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:36.010
How do you do it?

00:09:36.010 --> 00:09:39.730
So you will start evaluating
solutions, doing a scale

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:42.730
of one, 10, 20 workloads.

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:44.110
How do you do this at scale?

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:46.480
How do you go thousands of VMs?

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:49.150
How do you move a physical
data center to the cloud?

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:50.550
It's a hard thing to do.

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:53.830
It involves planning,
understanding

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:57.070
the framework, the architecture,
the networking, the security

00:09:57.070 --> 00:10:00.020
bits, and then being able
to do that on the cloud.

00:10:00.020 --> 00:10:03.820
So it's actually an extension
of your environment.

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:07.390
Multi-source-- well, there are
certain things that you cannot

00:10:07.390 --> 00:10:08.850
move to the cloud.

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:14.140
For legal reasons, for
practical reasons--

00:10:14.140 --> 00:10:15.850
there is a bunch of
different reasons

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:18.580
where you will
need to run, still,

00:10:18.580 --> 00:10:21.680
certain applications
on your data center,

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:22.870
at physical locations.

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:27.100
And they should co-exist
with the ones that you move.

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:30.580
And so therefore, when you
move certain things away,

00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:31.720
run them on the cloud.

00:10:31.720 --> 00:10:33.490
And they have to talk
to your on-premise.

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:37.150
You need to make sure that,
where they are, again, latency

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:43.180
is good enough, response time,
SLAs, and all these things.

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:45.050
So that's multi-source.

00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:47.830
Three, well, the
applications are

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:50.920
complex multi-tier applications.

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:56.240
If we look at databases, if we
look at services that you run,

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:58.900
typically they're
developed by someone,

00:10:58.900 --> 00:11:02.350
and then oftentimes you know
that the application is running

00:11:02.350 --> 00:11:04.570
and it's a complex
application solving

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:07.108
one of the things in your
environment, in your company,

00:11:07.108 --> 00:11:08.650
but you don't know
how to upgrade it,

00:11:08.650 --> 00:11:11.320
you don't know what it's doing.

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:13.480
And you still would
like to migrate it.

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:15.610
So you would like to have
tools that figure out

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:17.620
what the application
is, and then

00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:22.090
enable you to take that to the
cloud in a way that makes sense

00:11:22.090 --> 00:11:24.160
and to still have it functional.

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:29.830
So we also have that
part of capability

00:11:29.830 --> 00:11:33.460
with what we were looking
at, with Anthos Migrate.

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:36.290
And the last part is the risk.

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:38.560
So we oftentimes talk--

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:41.770
when we talk migration, we talk
about moving you to the cloud

00:11:41.770 --> 00:11:43.420
and migrating you.

00:11:43.420 --> 00:11:46.120
But one aspect that's
not often talked about

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:49.640
is, well, what do you
do if it doesn't work?

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:53.380
You want to make sure you can
actually go back on-premise.

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:56.770
And so the software for
migration that you look at,

00:11:56.770 --> 00:12:00.610
you want to have a seamless
way of actually moving

00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:04.900
to the cloud, but
also back on-premise.

00:12:04.900 --> 00:12:09.610
And so you want an environment
that gives you that flexibility

00:12:09.610 --> 00:12:13.420
and gives you the same time
frame or the same performance,

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:17.170
whether you go to the cloud
or whether you come back

00:12:17.170 --> 00:12:19.030
on-premise at any given time.

00:12:22.270 --> 00:12:28.530
So this is our solution when
we look at modernization

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:29.340
and how we do it.

00:12:29.340 --> 00:12:32.390
So what I will tell you
now is about our product,

00:12:32.390 --> 00:12:36.570
Anthos Migrate that gives
you this modernization

00:12:36.570 --> 00:12:38.520
aspect for your workloads.

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:41.670
And what we modernized is
that we take workloads running

00:12:41.670 --> 00:12:46.750
on bare-metal on
virtual machines,

00:12:46.750 --> 00:12:49.500
whether it's on your
on-premise or whether it's

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:51.150
on a different cloud.

00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:53.430
And how do we take
those workloads?

00:12:53.430 --> 00:12:55.060
Windows or Linux.

00:12:55.060 --> 00:12:57.450
And we move them over to GCP.

00:12:57.450 --> 00:12:59.670
And when it's Linux,
today, we actually

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:02.200
move them into containers.

00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:06.060
And so it's a one-step
process to get you directly

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:10.860
to the container journey,
and to get you to run on GKE.

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:15.420
And that's really the value
here, the leap of technology

00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:18.150
we're taking into
modernizing your VM

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:21.450
running somewhere,
and then ending up

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:26.365
as this VM runs in
a container on GKE.

00:13:26.365 --> 00:13:28.740
And then you can leverage all
the ecosystem of the things

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:32.340
that we talk about around GKE.

00:13:32.340 --> 00:13:34.180
So this is our vision.

00:13:34.180 --> 00:13:38.430
It's about migrating
and modernizing the VMs.

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:41.970
And the way we will take
you there is-- basically

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:45.900
it can be a one-step process or
it can be a multi-step process,

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:49.920
depending on how ready you
are and where in your journey

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:50.820
you are.

00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:52.860
And what you see
here on this graph,

00:13:52.860 --> 00:13:55.830
we start to read this graph
by the bottom left side, which

00:13:55.830 --> 00:13:57.180
is your starting point.

00:13:57.180 --> 00:13:59.970
You're either on-premise
or another cloud,

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:02.430
and you have a workload.

00:14:02.430 --> 00:14:07.530
Then you can actually go
directly and end your journey

00:14:07.530 --> 00:14:13.590
as running, on the top left
side, as a container on GKE.

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:16.050
You can do that directly
with Anthos Migrate,

00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:19.380
or we can do it also in a
two-step process with Anthos

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:20.370
Migrate.

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:25.740
So we can take you first to
run on, for example, GCP.

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:30.570
So we can get your VM to
run as a VM on Google Cloud,

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:34.030
and then modernize it, or we
can modernize it directly.

00:14:34.030 --> 00:14:37.000
So depending on where
you are in your journey,

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:38.790
you don't have to wait for this.

00:14:38.790 --> 00:14:41.100
You can start, and
we can meet you

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:43.740
in your journey of
modernization, where you are.

00:14:47.270 --> 00:14:49.040
Here is our approach.

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:55.280
And our approach is
this two possible phase.

00:14:55.280 --> 00:14:57.590
This is the journey
of a migration.

00:14:57.590 --> 00:15:00.500
And I'm trying to show
you here the lifecycle.

00:15:00.500 --> 00:15:04.490
So we start with a pre-sale,
first, environment,

00:15:04.490 --> 00:15:06.780
where when we have
conversations about migration,

00:15:06.780 --> 00:15:09.050
we talk about discovery.

00:15:09.050 --> 00:15:11.480
And what discovery
is is to figure out

00:15:11.480 --> 00:15:14.870
what your environment is,
what really is happening

00:15:14.870 --> 00:15:19.100
in your IT environment, what
applications are running, what

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:21.740
are the dependencies,
where are they located,

00:15:21.740 --> 00:15:24.590
what is their network, what
are the security policies,

00:15:24.590 --> 00:15:26.150
and so forth.

00:15:26.150 --> 00:15:28.890
Then comes the
migration planning,

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:33.420
which is still a more of a paper
phase or a discovery phase,

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:36.590
which we start thinking
about what are we going

00:15:36.590 --> 00:15:40.790
to migrate, when, and how.

00:15:40.790 --> 00:15:46.470
And then we go into our,
basically, next step,

00:15:46.470 --> 00:15:49.190
which is our migration practice.

00:15:49.190 --> 00:15:52.250
And what you see here
is two different trains

00:15:52.250 --> 00:15:53.570
that we can take.

00:15:53.570 --> 00:15:56.270
The top one that we run on GCE--

00:15:56.270 --> 00:16:00.440
so the top one is we take
a VM running on-premise,

00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:03.410
and then we go and run
that as a migration,

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:04.970
and we end up on GCE.

00:16:04.970 --> 00:16:08.210
So that's our
lift-and-shift technology.

00:16:08.210 --> 00:16:10.820
And at the bottom
one, you see our type

00:16:10.820 --> 00:16:13.340
of doing that with GKE.

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:17.240
So similar processes, but
it's just that we can adapt

00:16:17.240 --> 00:16:19.190
the journey to where you are.

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:20.930
And this is why we--

00:16:20.930 --> 00:16:24.720
this is what we offer
you with our software,

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:27.290
with Anthos Migrate.

00:16:27.290 --> 00:16:30.860
So I want to go through
the benefits of two things.

00:16:30.860 --> 00:16:33.750
I want to go over the
VM-to-VM migration,

00:16:33.750 --> 00:16:36.260
and then I'll tell you about
our VM-to-GKE migration, so

00:16:36.260 --> 00:16:39.860
the two different
aspects of our product.

00:16:39.860 --> 00:16:42.650
What you see with our
VM-to-VM migration

00:16:42.650 --> 00:16:46.120
is that we actually
want to move you.

00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:47.120
It's our lift and shift.

00:16:47.120 --> 00:16:49.070
We want to move
you to Google Cloud

00:16:49.070 --> 00:16:53.450
with a fast-paced technology.

00:16:53.450 --> 00:16:56.390
The way we achieve
that is we actually

00:16:56.390 --> 00:16:58.410
have a couple of key benefits.

00:16:58.410 --> 00:17:02.450
One is we have what we
call real-time streaming.

00:17:02.450 --> 00:17:04.069
With real-time
streaming, what we

00:17:04.069 --> 00:17:07.430
do is when start your
migration journey,

00:17:07.430 --> 00:17:10.900
see it as, when you're
running a VM on-premise,

00:17:10.900 --> 00:17:14.990
we start a VM on
Google Cloud, on GCP.

00:17:14.990 --> 00:17:17.240
So in about five to
10 minutes, depending

00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:21.050
if it's Windows or Linux,
we fire up the VM in GCP.

00:17:21.050 --> 00:17:25.190
The disk, the data, the
storage of your initial VM

00:17:25.190 --> 00:17:29.060
is basically streamed to your
destination VM on the cloud,

00:17:29.060 --> 00:17:32.120
and then we cache it
and we transfer the data

00:17:32.120 --> 00:17:34.020
in the background.

00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:36.740
So what this means
for you is that you

00:17:36.740 --> 00:17:41.300
actually, within minutes, know
if your migrated workload is

00:17:41.300 --> 00:17:43.340
actually operational.

00:17:43.340 --> 00:17:46.100
And you don't have to wait
for the whole migration

00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:49.100
to finish to then
test it and see

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:51.080
if it's actually functioning.

00:17:51.080 --> 00:17:54.440
So within minutes, you
can test the migration,

00:17:54.440 --> 00:17:57.470
and we can migrate
storage in the background.

00:17:57.470 --> 00:18:01.100
This allows us to
accelerate the migration,

00:18:01.100 --> 00:18:04.790
and really to make it
meaningful to you in a sense

00:18:04.790 --> 00:18:10.580
that you actually can understand
if your destination result will

00:18:10.580 --> 00:18:13.880
actually work with
what you want it to be.

00:18:13.880 --> 00:18:16.580
We don't need to install
agents on your VM.

00:18:16.580 --> 00:18:19.490
It's an agentless design.

00:18:19.490 --> 00:18:22.700
We can test before migration,
and we can roll back.

00:18:22.700 --> 00:18:26.330
So with these benefits what
you see on the chart is

00:18:26.330 --> 00:18:29.450
you can actually,
over time, migrate

00:18:29.450 --> 00:18:31.490
a lot more at-scale VMs.

00:18:31.490 --> 00:18:33.410
And this is real customer data.

00:18:33.410 --> 00:18:38.120
You can migrate, at scale,
thousands and thousands of VMs

00:18:38.120 --> 00:18:39.665
with our automated process.

00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:46.090
And scale of operations--

00:18:46.090 --> 00:18:49.230
another thing to show
you here is, the VMs,

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:53.100
so say we take a two-week
migration journey,

00:18:53.100 --> 00:18:57.420
which is fairly typical
for production migrations--

00:18:57.420 --> 00:19:00.640
first week is discovery, second
week is the implementation--

00:19:00.640 --> 00:19:03.210
you can actually see
that, in our second week,

00:19:03.210 --> 00:19:05.400
the migration completes very--

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:07.830
the VM starts
running right away,

00:19:07.830 --> 00:19:10.080
and then the data being
transferred in the background,

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:12.870
we're actually getting you
up and running very quickly

00:19:12.870 --> 00:19:16.087
in that second phase when
it's the real migration.

00:19:16.087 --> 00:19:17.670
And that's with the
technology that we

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:22.270
have behind Anthos Migrate.

00:19:22.270 --> 00:19:25.130
Now let's talk about
the second point.

00:19:25.130 --> 00:19:28.750
So I told you about
VM-to-VM migration.

00:19:28.750 --> 00:19:31.060
Now it's about VM to containers.

00:19:34.220 --> 00:19:35.860
So you're developing
applications,

00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:38.290
and your new applications
are, say, running on GKE

00:19:38.290 --> 00:19:39.880
or running as containers.

00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:42.190
But now you also have
legacy workloads.

00:19:42.190 --> 00:19:44.870
And for those, you don't
want to rewrite them.

00:19:44.870 --> 00:19:47.990
But if you can move them
to the cloud and on GKE,

00:19:47.990 --> 00:19:50.290
then you can instrument
them and interface them

00:19:50.290 --> 00:19:54.430
with the modern microservices
that you're building

00:19:54.430 --> 00:19:58.030
and take advantage of
the same benefits of GKE

00:19:58.030 --> 00:20:01.990
where we manage
the kernel for you.

00:20:01.990 --> 00:20:05.110
You have access to
our CI/CD pipeline

00:20:05.110 --> 00:20:10.150
where you can use other services
such as Istio and services mesh

00:20:10.150 --> 00:20:13.300
and interact with your
microservice applications.

00:20:13.300 --> 00:20:18.680
So we take those legacy apps
where rewrite is not an option,

00:20:18.680 --> 00:20:24.610
and we have it to run for
you in a container on GCP.

00:20:24.610 --> 00:20:26.470
Why wouldn't you just
do a lift and shift?

00:20:26.470 --> 00:20:28.390
Well, if you were just
doing lift and shift,

00:20:28.390 --> 00:20:30.310
and just moving it
as a VM, you would

00:20:30.310 --> 00:20:32.920
get some benefits,
but not as much

00:20:32.920 --> 00:20:38.350
as if you run as a
container, especially because

00:20:38.350 --> 00:20:42.320
of the modernization reasons
that we were talking about.

00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:46.540
And then, finally, we
don't see the migration

00:20:46.540 --> 00:20:47.510
as a binary problem.

00:20:50.320 --> 00:20:53.620
We have customers that
understand that containers,

00:20:53.620 --> 00:20:56.200
GKE, and so forth
is interesting,

00:20:56.200 --> 00:20:59.470
although they don't see all
the benefits right away.

00:20:59.470 --> 00:21:02.830
We can meet you where you are
in your migration journey,

00:21:02.830 --> 00:21:06.850
and we can take you through
that step forward to GKE.

00:21:06.850 --> 00:21:13.630
So it's a further toolbox
that you have for your app.

00:21:13.630 --> 00:21:17.680
And another point here is,
well, why do we do this?

00:21:17.680 --> 00:21:21.070
A big reason why this comes
back is customers love GKE.

00:21:21.070 --> 00:21:28.380
GKE is, today, a leading
container managed service

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:30.250
that a lot of our
customers just want

00:21:30.250 --> 00:21:32.740
to be on because of all
the other benefits we have

00:21:32.740 --> 00:21:36.190
once you are on GKE.

00:21:36.190 --> 00:21:39.130
And legacy apps, once
they're there, again,

00:21:39.130 --> 00:21:43.900
they're onboarded, you start
to see the benefit of it.

00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:47.520
So now think about if you
had to move two containers,

00:21:47.520 --> 00:21:49.950
your own apps, yourself, and
rewrite them or rebuild them,

00:21:49.950 --> 00:21:52.230
and you have to do that
at scale, it's probably

00:21:52.230 --> 00:21:55.710
not something that's going
to be either happening

00:21:55.710 --> 00:21:59.850
or that's going to be really
appealing for you to do,

00:21:59.850 --> 00:22:01.200
so they may end up staying.

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:04.020
So what we do with our
capability of running

00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:07.380
these legacy workloads--

00:22:07.380 --> 00:22:11.700
middleware, databases
and so forth--

00:22:11.700 --> 00:22:18.270
we enable you to meet us in
the cloud journey very quickly.

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:22.530
And we run these containers,
same as our VM-to-VM migration,

00:22:22.530 --> 00:22:26.290
about five minutes or so, you're
up and running with the data.

00:22:26.290 --> 00:22:28.980
What you saw today
