00:28:47.540 --> 00:28:49.430
And some of the
transformation capabilities

00:28:49.430 --> 00:28:52.070
we get from Velostrata.

00:28:52.070 --> 00:28:53.800
LUCIEN AVRAMOV:
Thank you, Michael.

00:28:53.800 --> 00:28:55.137
Walker, how about you?

00:28:55.137 --> 00:28:55.970
WALKER MILLER: Yeah.

00:28:55.970 --> 00:28:57.050
I'm Walker Miller.

00:28:57.050 --> 00:28:58.970
I'm from Cardinal Health.

00:28:58.970 --> 00:29:04.190
We are a global health
care, pharmaceutical,

00:29:04.190 --> 00:29:07.820
and medical supplies
manufacturer and distributor.

00:29:07.820 --> 00:29:10.700
We have been in the process
for the last couple of years

00:29:10.700 --> 00:29:16.130
of doing a massive migration
from on-premise to cloud,

00:29:16.130 --> 00:29:17.632
using--

00:29:17.632 --> 00:29:20.450
well, Velostrata, at the
time, is what it was.

00:29:20.450 --> 00:29:22.530
We are just about finished.

00:29:22.530 --> 00:29:26.030
We'll be done in July with most
of our production instances.

00:29:26.030 --> 00:29:30.110
And we've been experimenting
a bit with this new product,

00:29:30.110 --> 00:29:32.017
as well.

00:29:32.017 --> 00:29:33.100
LUCIEN AVRAMOV: Thank you.

00:29:33.100 --> 00:29:34.010
Luke?

00:29:34.010 --> 00:29:35.120
LUKE WEBB: I'm Luke Webb.

00:29:35.120 --> 00:29:38.060
I'm on the Cloud Enablement
Team with Tyson Foods.

00:29:38.060 --> 00:29:40.340
So we're a food company.

00:29:40.340 --> 00:29:43.070
We did about $40 billion
in sales last year

00:29:43.070 --> 00:29:49.190
across our four main BUs,
which are beef, poultry, pork,

00:29:49.190 --> 00:29:51.590
and prepared foods.

00:29:51.590 --> 00:29:56.150
So we operate across multiple
states and multiple countries.

00:29:56.150 --> 00:30:00.720
And our IT workforce is also
spread across those locations.

00:30:00.720 --> 00:30:03.770
We started a cloud migration
and a digital transformation

00:30:03.770 --> 00:30:05.580
about 18 months ago.

00:30:05.580 --> 00:30:07.740
And so we've been doing
a lot of discovery,

00:30:07.740 --> 00:30:11.060
a lot of testing,
a lot of deciding

00:30:11.060 --> 00:30:13.620
on how we want to do things.

00:30:13.620 --> 00:30:15.460
So we know how we
want to do things.

00:30:15.460 --> 00:30:16.880
And we just want
to figure out how

00:30:16.880 --> 00:30:18.980
to get there to that end state.

00:30:18.980 --> 00:30:25.590
So we have a pretty aggressive
cloud migration strategy.

00:30:25.590 --> 00:30:30.125
And Velostrata is going
to be part of that.

00:30:30.125 --> 00:30:33.010
LUCIEN AVRAMOV: Let's talk
a little bit about scale.

00:30:33.010 --> 00:30:37.930
And I think, Walker, you're
one of our lead examples.

00:30:37.930 --> 00:30:40.900
Tell us about scale,
in terms of how large,

00:30:40.900 --> 00:30:44.620
or where are you, in terms
of that journey of migrating?

00:30:44.620 --> 00:30:47.650
You've experienced our
product with migrating

00:30:47.650 --> 00:30:52.420
certain workloads and then
moving a higher amount.

00:30:52.420 --> 00:30:53.950
How has that process
been for you?

00:30:53.950 --> 00:30:57.130
And do you foresee a
similar type of activity

00:30:57.130 --> 00:30:59.530
for potentially containers?

00:30:59.530 --> 00:31:02.380
As you look at your
modernization journey,

00:31:02.380 --> 00:31:05.450
are you also
thinking maybe scale?

00:31:05.450 --> 00:31:09.707
How do you see that to fit
your model for your company?

00:31:09.707 --> 00:31:10.540
WALKER MILLER: Yeah.

00:31:10.540 --> 00:31:12.040
Well, I think when
we're done, we'll

00:31:12.040 --> 00:31:16.960
have migrated around
4,000 servers.

00:31:16.960 --> 00:31:19.060
We're probably about
75%, 80% done with that.

00:31:19.060 --> 00:31:20.970
We just got production
[? to knock ?] off--

00:31:20.970 --> 00:31:25.960
probably getting closer
to 85%, 90% here.

00:31:25.960 --> 00:31:29.440
So yeah, we've been doing
it over a couple of years.

00:31:29.440 --> 00:31:33.670
Spent a lot of time doing
discovery and analysis, which

00:31:33.670 --> 00:31:36.760
allowed us to be successful.

00:31:36.760 --> 00:31:39.130
As far as the container,
I'm not really sure

00:31:39.130 --> 00:31:40.790
yet what that's
going to look like.

00:31:40.790 --> 00:31:43.090
I think that's
kind of new to us.

00:31:43.090 --> 00:31:46.360
We've been doing it
internally with a few apps,

00:31:46.360 --> 00:31:48.850
just kind of building
cloud-native,

00:31:48.850 --> 00:31:50.080
just to get an idea.

00:31:50.080 --> 00:31:52.470
We'll be going live with our
first production Kubernetes

00:31:52.470 --> 00:31:55.270
app in May.

00:31:55.270 --> 00:31:57.550
So we're still kind
of early adopters

00:31:57.550 --> 00:32:00.080
in there, as well, for us.

00:32:00.080 --> 00:32:02.692
And I don't know
what the end game

00:32:02.692 --> 00:32:04.400
is going to look like
for that quite yet.

00:32:04.400 --> 00:32:05.950
But I have a feeling--

00:32:05.950 --> 00:32:08.010
an inkling-- that
containers are going

00:32:08.010 --> 00:32:11.140
to be a pretty integral part of
that for all of our technology

00:32:11.140 --> 00:32:11.870
staff.

00:32:11.870 --> 00:32:12.790
LUCIEN AVRAMOV: Mhm.

00:32:12.790 --> 00:32:15.090
Thank you.

00:32:15.090 --> 00:32:17.700
Michael, we talked about--
a little bit earlier,

00:32:17.700 --> 00:32:21.550
you were telling me about
migrations, in general,

00:32:21.550 --> 00:32:23.530
and containers.

00:32:23.530 --> 00:32:25.990
Do you foresee this
technology, that it's something

00:32:25.990 --> 00:32:28.630
that you'd like to
take for modernizing

00:32:28.630 --> 00:32:33.675
a lot of your customer breadth
or activities with containers?

00:32:33.675 --> 00:32:35.050
How do you see
our new technology

00:32:35.050 --> 00:32:38.410
with containers to play a
role for Atos, compared to,

00:32:38.410 --> 00:32:41.590
say, traditional lift
and shift or moving

00:32:41.590 --> 00:32:44.205
to the cloud activities
for basically your company?

00:32:44.205 --> 00:32:45.080
MICHAEL KOLLAR: Yeah.

00:32:45.080 --> 00:32:47.148
So we've built a
platform in partnership

00:32:47.148 --> 00:32:49.440
with Google that we call the
Orchestrated Hybrid Cloud.

00:32:49.440 --> 00:32:51.040
It's based on Kubernetes.

00:32:51.040 --> 00:32:52.630
And it's a
container-based platform

00:32:52.630 --> 00:32:55.990
for microservice applications--
microservice-based

00:32:55.990 --> 00:32:57.440
applications.

00:32:57.440 --> 00:33:00.010
One of the things we need to
do is take legacy applications

00:33:00.010 --> 00:33:01.990
at scale and move
them to containers

00:33:01.990 --> 00:33:03.948
and essentially break
down the dependencies

00:33:03.948 --> 00:33:05.740
that they had on
operating systems, VMWare,

00:33:05.740 --> 00:33:08.260
and some of the other
proprietary technologies

00:33:08.260 --> 00:33:09.250
that we had.

00:33:09.250 --> 00:33:12.220
So in order to facilitate that,
we needed a technology that

00:33:12.220 --> 00:33:14.410
allowed us to, one,
do that separation,

00:33:14.410 --> 00:33:17.470
but then also create what I
would say are smaller, more

00:33:17.470 --> 00:33:18.750
segmented apps--

00:33:18.750 --> 00:33:21.070
microservices-- onto
a modern platform

00:33:21.070 --> 00:33:23.950
that we could then integrate
and then orchestrate

00:33:23.950 --> 00:33:25.540
whether they be
on-prem, because we

00:33:25.540 --> 00:33:27.290
have a requirement--
some of our customers

00:33:27.290 --> 00:33:29.350
still want
infrastructure on-prem.

00:33:29.350 --> 00:33:31.580
Like it or not, they've got
regulatory requirements,

00:33:31.580 --> 00:33:32.750
things of that nature.

00:33:32.750 --> 00:33:35.260
But then also, provide the way
to scale it and integrate it

00:33:35.260 --> 00:33:38.540
with public cloud offerings
that make more sense for them.

00:33:38.540 --> 00:33:41.080
So for us, containerization
is critical to transform

00:33:41.080 --> 00:33:41.633
customers.

00:33:41.633 --> 00:33:44.050
And we have customers today
that are building cloud-native

00:33:44.050 --> 00:33:46.990
applications that way, and also
want to take some of what they

00:33:46.990 --> 00:33:48.910
would refer to as their
low-hanging fruit--

00:33:48.910 --> 00:33:50.620
tier three and tier four apps--

00:33:50.620 --> 00:33:52.990
that really don't make sense
to keep on-prem anymore--

00:33:52.990 --> 00:33:54.700
that they could just
move to the cloud

00:33:54.700 --> 00:33:56.530
and do that in a
container-based approach

00:33:56.530 --> 00:34:00.522
and take full advantage of
what Google has to offer there.

00:34:00.522 --> 00:34:03.410
LUCIEN AVRAMOV: Thank you.

00:34:03.410 --> 00:34:04.410
Luke, how about you?

00:34:04.410 --> 00:34:08.260
So for Tyson Foods, how do
you see that model to play?

00:34:08.260 --> 00:34:10.150
What type of
applications, potentially,

00:34:10.150 --> 00:34:13.090
do you see for migration,
in general, I would say,

00:34:13.090 --> 00:34:14.000
to the cloud?

00:34:14.000 --> 00:34:15.610
What are the top of minds?

00:34:15.610 --> 00:34:17.290
And what are you
thinking, in terms

00:34:17.290 --> 00:34:20.920
of containers and
migrations to that,

00:34:20.920 --> 00:34:23.863
in terms of type of workloads
that you're looking at?

00:34:23.863 --> 00:34:24.530
LUKE WEBB: Yeah.

00:34:24.530 --> 00:34:29.110
So everything in our data center
is on the block for migration

00:34:29.110 --> 00:34:31.000
to the cloud.

00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:35.050
And containers are definitely
part of our strategy.

00:34:35.050 --> 00:34:37.480
Anything cloud-native
that we're building,

00:34:37.480 --> 00:34:40.750
we're definitely pushing
development teams to use

00:34:40.750 --> 00:34:43.810
Kubernetes, microservices,
Cloud Functions--

00:34:43.810 --> 00:34:46.580
those kind of technologies.

00:34:46.580 --> 00:34:51.610
But as far as migrating, what we
have in our data centers today,

00:34:51.610 --> 00:34:55.270
we're not sure would all those
things fit into Kubernetes.

00:34:55.270 --> 00:34:59.170
So we have a lot
of ERP systems that

00:34:59.170 --> 00:35:00.320
maybe don't fit that model.

00:35:00.320 --> 00:35:04.760
But we do have some
web-hosted applications that

00:35:04.760 --> 00:35:06.010
definitely would fit in there.

00:35:06.010 --> 00:35:12.820
And we have a lot of
Windows VMs, which

00:35:12.820 --> 00:35:16.990
aren't on the beta
release of this software

00:35:16.990 --> 00:35:18.620
but are on the roadmap.

00:35:18.620 --> 00:35:21.830
So we're interested to see
what comes in that space,

00:35:21.830 --> 00:35:25.510
as far as Windows VM to
Kubernetes migration tools.

00:35:25.510 --> 00:35:26.760
LUCIEN AVRAMOV: Windows, yeah.

00:35:26.760 --> 00:35:27.470
OK.

00:35:27.470 --> 00:35:27.970
Great.

00:35:33.169 --> 00:35:36.690
In terms of, I would say,
challenges, what do you

00:35:36.690 --> 00:35:39.420
gentlemen see as potentially
current challenges

00:35:39.420 --> 00:35:41.490
for migration?

00:35:41.490 --> 00:35:46.020
What is it for you that's the
biggest challenge, I would say,

00:35:46.020 --> 00:35:49.070
or one of the biggest challenge
when you look at migrations?

00:35:49.070 --> 00:35:53.510
What are the blockers for you
today for your environments?

00:35:53.510 --> 00:35:56.010
MICHAEL KOLLAR: So I can give
you two that we struggle with.

00:35:56.010 --> 00:35:58.193
So embedded security
inside of apps

00:35:58.193 --> 00:35:59.610
is something that,
even when we do

00:35:59.610 --> 00:36:02.790
the discoveries and things like
that, we don't typically find.

00:36:02.790 --> 00:36:04.160
Someone did role-based access.

00:36:04.160 --> 00:36:06.270
You go back in time, the
products lack integrity

00:36:06.270 --> 00:36:08.970
and stuff like that, did
role provision dynamically

00:36:08.970 --> 00:36:10.105
for the user.

00:36:10.105 --> 00:36:11.730
So how that happens
in the public cloud

00:36:11.730 --> 00:36:13.227
is going to be
very different when

00:36:13.227 --> 00:36:15.060
we do that migration
because that service is

00:36:15.060 --> 00:36:15.930
no longer there.

00:36:15.930 --> 00:36:18.180
So we're going to have to
do something to recreate it.

00:36:18.180 --> 00:36:22.110
The second area that we
get a little hung up on is

00:36:22.110 --> 00:36:24.780
the ability to deal
with or roll back

00:36:24.780 --> 00:36:27.270
because the batch processes
we have to support

00:36:27.270 --> 00:36:28.950
are downstream dependencies.

00:36:28.950 --> 00:36:31.050
Sometimes when we
migrate an application

00:36:31.050 --> 00:36:33.570
or we containerize it, the
same way that that app got

00:36:33.570 --> 00:36:35.900
triggered previously
in its previous state,

00:36:35.900 --> 00:36:37.560
is no longer the
same way we would

00:36:37.560 --> 00:36:40.153
want to do it in the future,
because the legacy systems

00:36:40.153 --> 00:36:41.820
don't understand
event-based programming

00:36:41.820 --> 00:36:44.358
and things of that nature
that we do for the new world.

00:36:44.358 --> 00:36:45.900
So we have to look
at, how do we deal

00:36:45.900 --> 00:36:47.970
with keeping some
of those events,

00:36:47.970 --> 00:36:49.950
or externalizing
those events, to keep

00:36:49.950 --> 00:36:52.480
some of the systems that
don't come over in sync?

00:36:52.480 --> 00:36:56.070
So security and embedded
types of batch processes

00:36:56.070 --> 00:36:58.560
and legacy-type
integration approaches

00:36:58.560 --> 00:37:00.960
are some of the inhibitors
or blockers we see when

00:37:00.960 --> 00:37:03.542
we're migrating the containers.

00:37:03.542 --> 00:37:05.000
LUCIEN AVRAMOV:
Thank you, Michael.

00:37:05.000 --> 00:37:09.030
WALKER MILLER: Yeah, I would say
with the migration as a whole,

00:37:09.030 --> 00:37:11.520
our biggest problem was just
knowing what talks to what.

00:37:11.520 --> 00:37:14.740
So discovery was our
biggest challenge.

00:37:14.740 --> 00:37:18.240
It's just because of the nature
of on-prem and segregation

00:37:18.240 --> 00:37:18.840
of duties.

00:37:18.840 --> 00:37:21.063
You had application
teams that didn't really

00:37:21.063 --> 00:37:22.980
fully understand how
their applications worked

00:37:22.980 --> 00:37:24.420
with other things.

00:37:24.420 --> 00:37:26.610
So going through and
digging through all the data

00:37:26.610 --> 00:37:30.210
we had to in order to figure out
that was probably the biggest

00:37:30.210 --> 00:37:33.210
challenge we had-- clearly,
the biggest challenge we had.

00:37:33.210 --> 00:37:35.785
As far as moving
something to Kubernetes,

00:37:35.785 --> 00:37:37.410
the technology doesn't
scare me at all.

00:37:37.410 --> 00:37:41.068
The technology is pretty sound.

00:37:41.068 --> 00:37:43.110
We've been playing with
traditional [? Webster ?]

00:37:43.110 --> 00:37:46.200
application server
in Kubernetes.

00:37:46.200 --> 00:37:48.980
And even just building it
natively, it works well.

00:37:48.980 --> 00:37:49.980
That's not a real issue.

00:37:49.980 --> 00:37:51.870
If you're running,
at least on Linux,

00:37:51.870 --> 00:37:54.540
you're going to run in
a container pretty well.

00:37:54.540 --> 00:37:57.030
One of the reasons we like
Kubernetes over other options

00:37:57.030 --> 00:37:59.520
is that it allows you
to run in that way, too.

00:37:59.520 --> 00:38:02.110
You don't necessarily have
to make it cloud-native.

00:38:02.110 --> 00:38:04.140
Or you don't have to
be a microservice.

00:38:04.140 --> 00:38:06.070
You can be a traditional VM.

00:38:06.070 --> 00:38:06.998
So it works out.

00:38:06.998 --> 00:38:09.540
I think the biggest challenge,
for us, is going to be vendors

00:38:09.540 --> 00:38:11.310
and just dealing
with them and making

00:38:11.310 --> 00:38:14.730
sure they're willing to support
us running into a container

00:38:14.730 --> 00:38:15.230
environment.

00:38:15.230 --> 00:38:18.390
I liken it of the move
from physical to VMs years

00:38:18.390 --> 00:38:22.050
ago, which I had the
luxury of being around for.

00:38:22.050 --> 00:38:24.610
And when you went to a
lot of vendors and said,

00:38:24.610 --> 00:38:28.040
hey, we want to run this on
in a virtualized environment,

00:38:28.040 --> 00:38:30.300
they just said, no, we're
not going to support it.

00:38:30.300 --> 00:38:31.983
And eventually,
that turn happened.

00:38:31.983 --> 00:38:34.150
And I feel like that turn
will happen here, as well.

00:38:37.140 --> 00:38:39.530
LUKE WEBB: I would say,
probably resource constraints

00:38:39.530 --> 00:38:43.190
have been our biggest
challenge so far.

00:38:43.190 --> 00:38:45.620
Ever since we started
our cloud journey,

00:38:45.620 --> 00:38:48.140
we've been on a big project.

00:38:48.140 --> 00:38:50.570
We had acquired
another company that

00:38:50.570 --> 00:38:53.600
had their entire IT
infrastructure, their own ERP

00:38:53.600 --> 00:38:54.180
systems.

00:38:54.180 --> 00:38:58.190
So we've been
combining those two.

00:38:58.190 --> 00:39:00.600
We were founded in 1935.

00:39:00.600 --> 00:39:02.630
So we're definitely a
born analog company.

00:39:02.630 --> 00:39:05.750
And we have a lot
of old processes

00:39:05.750 --> 00:39:08.570
that got pushed
into an ERP system.

00:39:08.570 --> 00:39:11.740
Well, we modified
our ERP system.

00:39:11.740 --> 00:39:14.180
It's a highly customized system.

00:39:14.180 --> 00:39:17.940
So part of this journey is
just a digital transformation

00:39:17.940 --> 00:39:20.850
to use SAS software,
like SAS software,

00:39:20.850 --> 00:39:23.300
and not modify it
unless there's some sort

00:39:23.300 --> 00:39:29.840
of distinguishing feature
of Tyson operations.

00:39:29.840 --> 00:39:32.510
So really, a lot
of resources have

00:39:32.510 --> 00:39:34.590
been tied up in that effort.

00:39:34.590 --> 00:39:36.142
So resources, and
then skill set--

00:39:36.142 --> 00:39:37.850
when the resources
are tied up elsewhere,

00:39:37.850 --> 00:39:39.590
they can't develop
the cloud skills

00:39:39.590 --> 00:39:42.710
that they need to get
onto this new platform

00:39:42.710 --> 00:39:46.170
for our next generation
of applications.

00:39:46.170 --> 00:39:51.170
So a tool like VM to GKE is
definitely very attractive.

00:39:51.170 --> 00:39:54.560
If you can get a push-button
move into a new, more

00:39:54.560 --> 00:39:58.790
modern environment,
then you don't

00:39:58.790 --> 00:40:01.550
have to take all that
time from your developers

00:40:01.550 --> 00:40:05.360
and your IT team to get
them onto that new platform.

00:40:08.230 --> 00:40:10.890
LUCIEN AVRAMOV: So
security is one aspect.

00:40:10.890 --> 00:40:15.340
Another aspect is
basically talking about--

00:40:15.340 --> 00:40:18.160
you mentioned about
support for application

00:40:18.160 --> 00:40:20.140
from vendors when
you go to the cloud

00:40:20.140 --> 00:40:22.120
and how are you supported.

00:40:22.120 --> 00:40:25.300
And another one is obviously
what you mentioned, as well,

00:40:25.300 --> 00:40:28.030
with the various things
that you have to manage,

00:40:28.030 --> 00:40:32.230
and then on-boarding
them to the cloud.

00:40:32.230 --> 00:40:33.770
What we seem to see
is a few things.

00:40:33.770 --> 00:40:40.270
So one is, I think that
what you brought up,

00:40:40.270 --> 00:40:43.060
Walker, about the
support for certain

00:40:43.060 --> 00:40:44.150
supports on containers--

00:40:44.150 --> 00:40:48.430
I think this is more
of a time constraint

00:40:48.430 --> 00:40:51.790
that's going to ease up
because I think it's something

00:40:51.790 --> 00:40:54.880
that we see, that everyone
wants to move to containers.

00:40:54.880 --> 00:40:58.300
So I think we start to
see various companies more

00:40:58.300 --> 00:40:59.570
open on the support model.

00:40:59.570 --> 00:41:03.700
So I think that's more on
the, I would say, support.

00:41:03.700 --> 00:41:05.350
And it's very important
for production.

00:41:05.350 --> 00:41:07.780
But I think we'll get there.

00:41:07.780 --> 00:41:12.370
In terms of security and what
we do once you're containers,

00:41:12.370 --> 00:41:14.860
I think that what
we're now working

00:41:14.860 --> 00:41:17.435
on Istio with the
service mesh is

00:41:17.435 --> 00:41:19.060
going to be really
compelling, in terms

00:41:19.060 --> 00:41:21.640
of adding more
security, and then

00:41:21.640 --> 00:41:23.440
with [INAUDIBLE]
and identity, to be

00:41:23.440 --> 00:41:25.160
able to basically have a user.

00:41:25.160 --> 00:41:28.000
So if you are on the
Active Directory Windows

00:41:28.000 --> 00:41:31.270
type of environment,
now you can actually--

00:41:31.270 --> 00:41:34.840
if you take our Anthos
migrate software now,

00:41:34.840 --> 00:41:39.130
we can actually
directly map to users

00:41:39.130 --> 00:41:47.155
that are in ADs to be moved to
containers to GKE with the IM

00:41:47.155 --> 00:41:49.120
settings of GCP.

00:41:49.120 --> 00:41:52.030
So I think the security
aspect is definitely key,

00:41:52.030 --> 00:41:55.390
because we want to make
sure we conserve that.

00:41:55.390 --> 00:41:57.760
But that's a really
interesting problem

00:41:57.760 --> 00:41:59.800
that we're looking to solve.

00:41:59.800 --> 00:42:02.860
And then you mentioned, Luke, I
think also Windows and Windows

00:42:02.860 --> 00:42:03.400
containers.

00:42:03.400 --> 00:42:05.290
So Windows is a big
thing, especially

00:42:05.290 --> 00:42:07.330
for legacy applications.

00:42:07.330 --> 00:42:10.270
This comes oftentimes--
well, I'd love to go to GKE,

00:42:10.270 --> 00:42:13.750
but I have Windows, so
what do I do with Windows?

00:42:13.750 --> 00:42:16.053
We're actually working
on the Windows part.

00:42:16.053 --> 00:42:17.720
This is something you
will see later on.

00:42:17.720 --> 00:42:19.310
It's on our roadmap.

00:42:19.310 --> 00:42:21.800
We started now with
Windows and containers.

00:42:21.800 --> 00:42:26.290
So we're starting to have
alpha and early access

00:42:26.290 --> 00:42:27.560
type of things.

00:42:27.560 --> 00:42:30.550
So I think the Windows part is
something that we're definitely

00:42:30.550 --> 00:42:33.140
aware on and working on.

00:42:33.140 --> 00:42:36.060
But in terms of getting
into containers,

00:42:36.060 --> 00:42:40.360
our software really has
the value of taking your VM

00:42:40.360 --> 00:42:43.720
and booting it up as
a StatefulSet on GKE.

00:42:43.720 --> 00:42:47.830
So we're not yet at a stage
where we break an application

00:42:47.830 --> 00:42:49.607
into microservices.

00:42:49.607 --> 00:42:51.190
But the first step
of what we're doing

00:42:51.190 --> 00:42:57.310
is, we take your legacy VM,
and then we get that to boot up

00:42:57.310 --> 00:42:58.310
and running a container.

00:42:58.310 --> 00:43:01.030
And then it can start
interface with other things.

00:43:01.030 --> 00:43:03.670
And at the same time,
once it's on containers,

00:43:03.670 --> 00:43:05.230
you actually get more scale.

00:43:05.230 --> 00:43:06.730
So you could actually
get a lot more

00:43:06.730 --> 00:43:10.450
workloads to run on a GKE
cluster versus, say, a VM

00:43:10.450 --> 00:43:11.000
cluster.

00:43:11.000 --> 00:43:16.120
So these were kind of
things where we are.

00:43:16.120 --> 00:43:19.030
What I wanted to ask
you guys is, maybe,

00:43:19.030 --> 00:43:20.980
what would be a one
top-of-mind thing

00:43:20.980 --> 00:43:23.140
that you would like
to see in the product?

00:43:23.140 --> 00:43:26.290
Or what are the things
that you're thinking of--

00:43:26.290 --> 00:43:28.570
once you're in
containers, what would you

00:43:28.570 --> 00:43:31.300
like to see that may
be missing or that

00:43:31.300 --> 00:43:33.340
is top-of-mind for
you as a next step

00:43:33.340 --> 00:43:39.220
to maybe get you more towards
a container environment?

00:43:39.220 --> 00:43:40.330
Anyone?

00:43:40.330 --> 00:43:42.940
LUKE WEBB: Well, I think you
just touched on it for us.

00:43:42.940 --> 00:43:47.590
So getting a VM or a legacy app
up into containers is great.

00:43:47.590 --> 00:43:50.320
But it doesn't break it down
into a microservices-type

00:43:50.320 --> 00:43:51.410
architecture for you.

00:43:51.410 --> 00:43:55.510
So just like Walker
mentioned, that the discovery

00:43:55.510 --> 00:43:56.410
is the hardest part.

00:43:56.410 --> 00:43:59.050
So anything that a tool
could do to help you

00:43:59.050 --> 00:44:02.860
discover how an application
talks to other applications

00:44:02.860 --> 00:44:06.015
and what databases
it is required of--

00:44:06.015 --> 00:44:07.890
those kind of things
would really be helpful.

00:44:09.823 --> 00:44:11.740
WALKER MILLER: The full
integration with Istio

00:44:11.740 --> 00:44:12.573
is going to be nice.

00:44:12.573 --> 00:44:14.980
Again, and the
idea of discovery--

00:44:14.980 --> 00:44:17.200
if we had had a service,
much like Istio, in place--

00:44:17.200 --> 00:44:19.955
or any service mesh in
place, for that matter--

00:44:19.955 --> 00:44:21.580
that would have made
that a lot easier,

00:44:21.580 --> 00:44:24.853
because we would have been
able to see those interactions

00:44:24.853 --> 00:44:26.020
a lot better than we do now.

00:44:26.020 --> 00:44:29.320
So service mesh is kind of
high on our list for just

00:44:29.320 --> 00:44:31.955
kind of all of our workloads.

00:44:31.955 --> 00:44:33.830
MICHAEL KOLLAR: And I
would like to also see,

00:44:33.830 --> 00:44:35.770
given that we spend a lot of
times integrating with legacy

00:44:35.770 --> 00:44:37.750
systems with our
customers, understanding

00:44:37.750 --> 00:44:40.390
how we can provide the mapping
back to the legacy within it

00:44:40.390 --> 00:44:42.370
as part of the discovery
process to keep

00:44:42.370 --> 00:44:44.850
those two worlds in sync as
we progress them forward.

00:44:48.350 --> 00:44:53.660
LUCIEN AVRAMOV: So yeah, moving
basically integration with CSM,

00:44:53.660 --> 00:44:57.890
how to move these apps, and
how do we do microservices--

00:44:57.890 --> 00:45:01.670
so the microservices part is
something that we're thinking

00:45:01.670 --> 00:45:03.788
to do--

00:45:03.788 --> 00:45:05.720
it's in our ideas.

00:45:05.720 --> 00:45:07.610
And that's something
hard to do is now,

00:45:07.610 --> 00:45:11.690
once you are on a container and
you run as a StatefulSet, well,

00:45:11.690 --> 00:45:14.450
you'd like that to
become microservices.

00:45:14.450 --> 00:45:17.150
So how do we break that
monolith application

00:45:17.150 --> 00:45:20.390
that's stateful to get you
to a stateless, server-less,

00:45:20.390 --> 00:45:24.030
completely autonomous
type of application?

00:45:24.030 --> 00:45:27.620
So that's also something that
is part of what we're thinking

00:45:27.620 --> 00:45:29.870
about, in terms of solving.

00:45:29.870 --> 00:45:33.500
But now we're looking at
basically taking your own GKE

00:45:33.500 --> 00:45:35.320
and moving forward with that.

00:45:35.320 --> 00:45:37.430
So I'm personally very
excited to collaborate

00:45:37.430 --> 00:45:41.390
with you guys on
this and for you

00:45:41.390 --> 00:45:43.430
to be our partners
on the journey,

00:45:43.430 --> 00:45:47.530
as we started all this
testing and product.

00:45:47.530 --> 00:45:50.305
So we'll continue, basically,
that and iterate on it.

00:45:54.560 --> 00:45:55.700
That makes sense.

00:45:55.700 --> 00:45:59.680
It's taking VMs, getting
people in containers,

00:45:59.680 --> 00:46:02.450
scaling it out at big scale--

00:46:02.450 --> 00:46:04.890
thousands and thousands
of workloads--

00:46:04.890 --> 00:46:08.420
and then giving you the
flexibility of the cloud.

00:46:08.420 --> 00:46:12.070
[MUSIC PLAYING]

